Now I understand the structure. The capfd capture uses `FDCapture` which uses `EncodedFile` wrapping a `TemporaryFile(buffering=0)` (a binary file). The `EncodedFile` is an `io.TextIOWrapper` created with default newline handling. When text is written to it and then read back with `.read()`, the `TextIOWrapper`'s default newline translation (`newline=None`) translates `\r` to `\n` on read, or more specifically, when reading in text mode with newline=None, universal newlines mode translates `\r\n` to `\n` and also `\r` to `\n`.

Actually the issue: `TemporaryFile(buffering=0)` - buffering=0 means unbuffered binary mode. Then it's wrapped in TextIOWrapper. When we write `'Greetings from DOS\r'` to the TemporaryFile via the fd (os.write), then read back via `self.tmpfile.read()`, the TextIOWrapper reads the bytes `\r` and translates it to `\n` because newline=None (universal newline mode).

Before the change (commit 29e4cb5d), the code used `safe_text_dupfile()` which probably opened the file with `newline=""` so no translation occurred. Now `EncodedFile(TemporaryFile(buffering=0), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", write_through=True)` doesn't pass `newline=""`, so the default newline=None applies.

